---
title: "Automating Your Inbox: Build an AI Email Gatekeeper, Step by Step"
---

For most small business owners, the inbox is a source of constant anxiety. It's a place where potential customers get lost, where "urgent" requests from friends compete with "important" invoices from vendors, and where hours of your day disappear into the "drudge work" of sorting and triaging.

In 2026, the solution isn't a better filter or a faster reply—it's an **AI Email Gatekeeper**.

### The Concept: From Triage to Action
Instead of you opening every email to decide what matters, an AI gatekeeper sits between your inbox and your attention. It doesn't just move emails into folders; it analyzes the *intent* of each message.

When an email arrives, the gatekeeper asks itself: "What is this person trying to achieve?"

- **Is it a scheduling request?** The gatekeeper checks your calendar, proposes three available slots, and drafts the reply.
- **Is it a pricing inquiry?** The gatekeeper pulls the current pricing from your database and prepares a personalized quote.
- **Is it a customer complaint?** The gatekeeper flags this as "High Priority" and immediately pings you on Slack.

In this guide, we'll build one concrete gatekeeper together: **every new Gmail is read by an AI, categorized (Scheduling / Pricing / Support / Personal), logged in a Google Sheet, and routed to Slack with the right priority.** No code, one afternoon of your time. Follow along with Zapier open in your browser and you'll have a working gatekeeper by the end.

### Before You Start
1.  **A Zapier account** at zapier.com. (The free plan works for a simple single-branch gatekeeper; a paid plan unlocks multi-branch routing.)
2.  **Gmail** connected to the same account you use for business email.
3.  **A Google Sheet** to serve as your "inbox log" — create one at docs.google.com with columns: `Date`, `Sender`, `Subject`, `Category`, `Priority`, `Notes`.
4.  **Slack** with a channel (e.g., `#inbox-alerts`) where you want the notifications to land.
5.  **An LLM step** — Zapier's built-in **AI** step (which uses a model behind the scenes). If you want a specific model, you can also wire in your own via an HTTP Request step to a provider like OpenAI, Anthropic, or an Ollama endpoint. For this guide, we'll use Zapier's built-in AI step so you don't need an API key.

### The Gatekeeper: Gmail → AI Categorize → Sheet + Slack

**Step 1: Create the Zap and pick your trigger app.**
Log in to Zapier and click **Create a Zap**. On the "What makes this Zap start?" screen, type `gmail` and select **Gmail**. This is your trigger — the moment a new email lands.

**Step 2: Choose the trigger event.**
Select **New Email**. (If you want to narrow it down, you can also pick "New Email with Specific Label" and pick, say, `Inbox` or a label you use for business mail.)

**Step 3: Connect your Gmail account.**
Zapier opens a Google permission page. Click **Allow** to let Zapier read your Gmail. Back in Zapier, you'll see the account confirmed with a green checkmark.

**Step 4: Set the scope.**
Zapier asks which mailbox to watch. Pick your business inbox. Optionally add a **Filter** so the gatekeeper only looks at, say, senders *not* on your contacts list, or emails that don't contain "newsletter" in the subject. This keeps the AI from burning tokens on junk mail.

**Step 5: Test the trigger.**
Zapier fetches a sample email so you can see the fields it will receive: `From`, `Subject`, `Body`, `Timestamp`, `Label Names`. If your inbox is empty, send yourself a test email first, then click **Continue**.

**Step 6: Add the AI step.**
Click **+ Add Step** (or "Add an action"). Search for `AI` and select **AI** (Zapier's built-in AI action). Choose the action **Generate Text**. This is where the "reasoning" happens.

**Step 7: Write the system prompt.**
This is the heart of the gatekeeper. In the **System Prompt** field, paste something like:

> *You are an executive assistant triaging a small-business owner's email. Read the email below and categorize it into exactly one of: `Scheduling`, `Pricing`, `Support`, `Personal`, or `Other`. Then assign a priority: `High`, `Medium`, or `Low`. `High` means it requires action within the hour (complaints, urgent scheduling, pricing for a named prospect). Output your answer as a single line of JSON with no other text:*
>
> *`{"category": "...", "priority": "...", "reason": "one short sentence"}`*

In the **User Prompt** field, plug in the live email data using the field picker:

> *Email from: {{From}}
> Subject: {{Subject}}
> Body: {{Body}}
>
> Categorize and assign priority.*

This prompt is doing three jobs at once: it picks a category, sets a priority, and forces a machine-readable JSON answer so the next steps can branch on it reliably.

**Step 8: Test the AI step.**
Click **Test this step**. Zapier runs the prompt against your sample email and shows you the JSON it produced — e.g., `{"category": "Pricing", "priority": "High", "reason": "Named prospect asks for quote"}`. If the output isn't valid JSON or the category is wrong, tweak the prompt (be more specific about the allowed categories, or add a "default to Other when unsure" instruction) and test again.

**Step 9: Add the Google Sheets action.**
Click **+ Add Step**, search for `sheets`, select **Google Sheets**, and choose **Create Spreadsheet Row**. Connect your Google account, pick the Sheet you made in "Before You Start," and map the columns:

- `Date` → `{{Timestamp}}`
- `Sender` → `{{From}}`
- `Subject` → `{{Subject}}`
- `Category` → the AI output (field picker from the AI step, e.g., `{{category}}`)
- `Priority` → the AI output (`{{priority}}`)
- `Notes` → the AI's reason (`{{reason}}`)

This gives you a running, searchable log of every email the gatekeeper has touched — invaluable when you want to review what the AI decided last week.

**Step 10: Test the Sheets step.**
Click **Test this step**. A new row appears in your Sheet with the sample email's data. Confirm the Category and Priority columns match what the AI said in Step 8.

**Step 11: Add the Slack action (with a filter).**
Click **+ Add Step**, search for `slack`, select **Slack**, and choose **Send Channel Message**. Connect your Slack workspace and pick the `#inbox-alerts` channel. Before you write the message, add a **Filter** so you only get pinged when it matters:

- **Filter 1:** `Priority` *equals* `High` → post a message.
- *(Optional, paid plan)* **Filter 2:** `Category` *equals* `Support` → post a different message.

In the **Message** field for the High-priority branch, map in the details:

> *🚨 High-priority email from {{From}}
> Subject: {{Subject}}
> Category: {{category}} — {{reason}}*

**Step 12: Test the Slack step (and the filter).**
Click **Test this step**. If the sample email was High priority, you'll see the message land in `#inbox-alerts`. If it wasn't High, the filter correctly *skips* the Slack step and you'll see a "Skipped" note in the Zap's run history. Both outcomes are correct behavior.

**Step 13: Name it and turn it on.**
Give the Zap a name like `Gmail → AI Gatekeeper → Sheet + Slack`. Flip the **Zap switch to On**. From this moment forward, every new email flows through the AI, gets logged, and pings you only when it's actually urgent.

### How to Verify It's Really Working
1.  Send yourself three test emails: one that's clearly a **scheduling request**, one that's a **pricing question**, and one that's **personal**.
2.  Wait ~30 seconds, then check your Google Sheet — there should be three new rows with sensible categories and priorities.
3.  Check Slack — you should have gotten **zero** pings if none of the three were High priority. If one was, you should have exactly one alert with the right subject and reason.
4.  Open the Zap's **Task History** tab. Every run should show all three steps green (or the Slack step shown as "Skipped" by the filter). If any step shows an error, click it to see the detail.

### Troubleshooting the Common Stumbles
*   **AI returns invalid JSON** — The model occasionally adds commentary. Make the prompt stricter: "Output *only* the JSON object, no markdown, no explanation." If it keeps failing, add a **Formatter → Text → Extract Data** step between the AI and Sheets to pull the JSON out of the raw output.
*   **AI categorizes everything as "Other"** — Your prompt is too vague. Give the model one or two concrete examples of each category in the system prompt (few-shot). E.g., "Example Scheduling: 'Can we meet Thursday?'"
*   **Sheet row is blank in a column** — The field name changed between the AI output and the Sheet mapping. Re-open the Sheets step and re-pick the correct field from the dropdown.
*   **Slack pings for every email** — Your filter is inverted or missing. Re-open the Slack step, confirm the Filter condition is `Priority` *equals* `High`, not "does not equal."
*   **Gmail asks you to re-approve** — Permissions can expire. Just re-approve; the Zap structure is preserved.
*   **The AI is slow or rate-limited** — Zapier's free plan has a daily cap on AI steps. If you're getting "rate limit" errors, either upgrade the plan or move the AI call to your own Ollama/OpenAI endpoint via an HTTP Request step.

### Where to Go Next
Once this basic gatekeeper is running, the interesting upgrades are:

*   **Draft the reply, not just the log.** Add a second AI step that writes a first-draft reply for Scheduling and Pricing categories, then a Gmail **Create Draft** action so the draft sits in your Drafts folder ready for a quick review-and-send.
*   **Route by category, not just priority.** Use a **Paths** step (paid plan) so Scheduling emails go to a calendar-availability check, Pricing emails go to a Google Sheets lookup of your rate card, and Support emails go straight to Slack with the full body.
*   **Add a "Human-in-the-loop" step.** For High-priority emails, instead of just pinging Slack, have Zapier create a **HubSpot/Notion task** assigned to you with a 1-hour due time, so the follow-up is tracked and can't be forgotten.
*   **Train the gatekeeper over time.** Review your Sheet weekly. When the AI gets a category wrong, copy the email's text into your prompt as a counter-example. Within a few weeks your gatekeeper will be sharper than any rule-based filter you could write by hand.

**The takeaway:** The gatekeeper is just a three-part pattern — *read the email, ask an AI what it wants, act on the answer* — and that pattern scales. Today it logs emails and pings you. Next month it's drafting replies and booking meetings. The inbox stops being a place you react to and becomes a system you steer.
